#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 960.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074759} :activity/id #uuid "f6ae1858-5ee1-5d31-814d-c2d09d20e0b8" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050409 :topic/id #uuid "46beb5e4-992f-546d-8051-796c949740a1" :topic/math-academy-id 526} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074759 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186075026 :tutorial/content "The shortest distance between a point and a line is the length of the line segment that connects the point to the line and is perpendicular to the line.\n\nFor example, consider the point ![](images/e2/e2002597cf3d8b01e24e7b4d2c6daf6fc9afb0afb3c6140877abfc3bf1be02c4.svg) and the horizontal line ![](images/06/06444505f504ecd01a91d4846b46bf202caafa9d9f756958483262f69b5db14b.svg) shown below:\n\n![](images/8c/8cba6df956e809981ac4a3ae78a6f6843be0ca8561fbcadd9d9ba98f2a5325cc.png)\n\nTo find the distance between the point and the line, we construct a segment with its endpoints on the point and the line, and which is perpendicular to the line\n\n![](images/80/802b30a788850457206716430755da2d5e3c6b3a53633c95626e49ee668d2507.png)\n\nThe distance between the point and the line is the length of this segment, which we compute as the absolute value of the difference in ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-coordinates of the point and the line:\n\n![](images/11/11e3682c2b811da299d9c475bd0ba3684d0c7f68e5677d925744b37026c47b0c.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "1d8ad60c-5b38-530d-abc1-d03020dca8b2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7819 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "be614ac7-a2e7-5e60-8bca-ad9de9ca9731" :step/math-academy-id 45229 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074760}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074760 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073810 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073434 :question/id #uuid "2ce03c34-8e2c-5554-a129-1eaf05ad46fd" :question/math-academy-id "e-12548"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "522b66f4-8a46-5dd0-a9d7-3b081c0ed157" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049868}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488349} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074509} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074510} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250241} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342181} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342182} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342183} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541082}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distance Between a Point and a Horizontal or Vertical Line"} :step/id #uuid "9f8070e4-2aac-5a71-be57-98f43a5de8dc" :step/math-academy-id 45230 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074761}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074761 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186075027 :tutorial/content "Consider the line $l$ with the equation $y=-x + 6$ and the point $Q(2,1)$ that does not lie on $l.$\n\n![](images/83/8353825890413432e74d30512219cb95edc76fe8c24e7a824d6893c745c48bd1.png)\n\nSuppose we wish to find the point $P$ that lies on $l$ that's closest to the point $(2,1).$ \n\n![](images/f8/f880cfde6bdfe07c6804609f9b052189632afcde4907207de5245298aea9ea6e.png)\n\nNow, here's the key idea: $P$ is the point that lies on $l$ *and* lies on the line *perpendicular* to $l$ that passes through $Q.$\n\nSo, to find $P,$ we proceed as follows:\n\n- First, we find the line perpendicular to $l$ that passes through $Q.$\n\n- Then, we find the intersection of the two lines.\n\nThe line $y=-x + 6$ has a slope of $-1,$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $1$ (the negative reciprocal). It also must pass through the point $(2,1).$ So, using point-slope form, we find that the equation of the perpendicular line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =1\\cdot (x-2) \\\\ y-1 & =x-2 \\\\ y & =x-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find where the lines $y=-x+6$ and $y=x-1$ intersect, we set the equations equal to each other and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x+6 & =x-1 \\\\ 6+1 & =x+x \\\\ 7 & =2x \\\\ x & =\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\dfrac{7}{2}$ into either of the two equations gives $y=\\dfrac{5}{2}.$ So, the coordinates of $P$ are $\\left( \\dfrac{7}{2}, \\dfrac{5}{2} \\right).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "8fd7f6d4-4f53-50f8-9d72-cceb3d3315ec" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7820 :tutorial/title "Finding the Point on a Line Closest to Another Point"} :step/id #uuid "e4482ee1-af85-5a92-b2a1-929166abc8b4" :step/math-academy-id 45233 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074767}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074762 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073811 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073435 :question/id #uuid "64a6782b-aa64-5027-8311-3498c19c39e1" :question/math-academy-id "e-12549"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7030a12-bd18-5f8b-84eb-28b6f216f972" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050975}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484391} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074511} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074512} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234360} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250246} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250247} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250253} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250254} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342208} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342209} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342211} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484379} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484383} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484390} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541072} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541103}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distance Between a Point and a Line"} :step/id #uuid "37db8424-1ef2-55d0-b76f-cd55f5ac5469" :step/math-academy-id 45234} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074767 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073815 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073436 :question/id #uuid "57fc631b-c251-55d2-a9d5-6b41fc4bb00c" :question/math-academy-id "e-12612"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "59d3a391-c163-5a04-944c-5d61a3950e03" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049869} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050473}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488393} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074513} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074514} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250245} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250248} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250249} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250250} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342210} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342212} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488386} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488392} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541092}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Point on a Line That Is Closest to a Given Point"} :step/id #uuid "14381924-124a-5e92-85f9-6c4bc617cf97" :step/math-academy-id 45522 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074768}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074768 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186075028 :tutorial/content "Earlier, we found that the point $P\\left(\\dfrac72,\\dfrac52\\right)$ lies on the line $y=-x+6$ and is the closest point on this line to the point $Q(2,1).$\n\n![](images/11/11901390427d5cd79af514a589175044cafebf47becdae84045005f4564de090.png)\n\nTo calculate the shortest *distance* between $Q$ and the line, we compute the distance between $P$ and $Q$ using the distance formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})}^{2}+{({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{7}{2}-2)}^{2}+{(\\frac{5}{2}-1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{4}+\\frac{9}{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shortest distance between the line $y=-x+6$ and $Q(2,1)$ is $\\dfrac{ 3 }{ \\sqrt{2} } .$" :tutorial/id #uuid "fb4d84ad-bfc1-5e9f-9da6-1447abdc11bc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7916 :tutorial/title "The Shortest Distance Between a Point and a Line"} :step/id #uuid "a2673e24-58fd-5d2a-98b3-03e91da98a5b" :step/math-academy-id 45600 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074762}}] :activity/title "The Shortest Distance Between a Point and a Line" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186073946}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561089} :activity/id #uuid "0ac10cd6-87b4-5be4-8d2a-8b946ef63557" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050409 :topic/id #uuid "46beb5e4-992f-546d-8051-796c949740a1" :topic/math-academy-id 526} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561074 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342211 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3cdc61b3-8b79-5ea8-a619-8426af491629" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339670} {:answer/id #uuid "67f37f95-6de1-5d04-b71e-ed84f5ca8a57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340056} {:answer/id #uuid "ab06bd68-2d98-5de6-8d36-5e85b3672d7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340684} {:answer/id #uuid "c3ad83ea-b045-52c1-bf05-b58e0937ea33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340869} {:answer/id #uuid "e8141639-7376-52d2-962a-0f9bff8db53d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341176}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341176} :answer-field/id #uuid "e40ef719-3a96-5536-9367-e13d22ca4b80" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341785}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "5a03ec8c-4418-5e4a-aaf2-129c88104ba7" :question/math-academy-id "q-74435" :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the point $(-3,-6)$ and the line $y+2x=-2.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the equation of the given line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+2x & =-2 \\\\ y & =-2x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's draw a diagram describing our situation.\n\n![](images/78/789659bd700bc5f8407d409bc28bf5ce5309369af2aa5ad5973434d47cff74fa.png)\n\n \n\nNext, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=-2x-2$ and passes through the point $(-3,-6).$\n\n The given line has a slope of $-2,$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y+6 & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+3) \\\\ y+6 & =\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=-2x-2 \\\\ y &=\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x-2 & =\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ -2x-\\frac{1}{2}x & =2-\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2}x & =-\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=1$ into either of the two equations gives $y=-4.$ So, our solution is $x=1$ and $y=-4.$ So, the lines intersect at the point $(1,-4).$\n\nFinally, we find the distance $d$ between the points $(-3,-6)$ and $(1,-4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(1-(-3))}^{2}+(-4-(-6))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{20} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the shortest distance between the point $(-3,-6)$ and the line $y+2x=-2$ is $2\\sqrt{5}.$"} :step/id #uuid "581eac97-d55b-546b-8351-221450ce3692" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561096}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561089 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342209 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19034fbf-ecd9-55d6-861b-0d5e5661524d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339349} {:answer/id #uuid "2ddbc882-a5c7-5c65-8d64-d4d3043edf48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339533} {:answer/id #uuid "4add45f8-1363-5fe9-be2e-64e9fe43e788" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339801} {:answer/id #uuid "83bfa442-6fa5-5f09-aec0-57fb97c3556e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340313} {:answer/id #uuid "b1385a23-f178-5808-937d-bee3d508b96e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340740}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339533} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f5e6af3-06a0-5b37-9c66-995c74e7a84d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341663}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ac99e412-cc1f-5160-a402-dce6424f38c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-74420" :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the point $(5,-8)$ and the line $y=\\frac{1}{3}x-3.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's draw a diagram describing our situation.\n\n![](images/6e/6ec04fe0ad996f87dbc07b0883afb3fa7aef1429412a550afe9102d1c22db71b.png)\n\n  \n\nFirst, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=\\frac{1}{3}x-3$ and passes through the point $(5,-8).$\n\n The given line has a slope of $\\frac{1}{3},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-3.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y+8 & =-3(x-5) \\\\ y+8 & =-3x+15 \\\\ y & =-3x+7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=\\frac{1}{3}x-3 \\\\ y &=-3x+7\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}x-3 & =-3x+7 \\\\ 3x+\\frac{1}{3}x & =7+3 \\\\ \\frac{10}{3}x & =10 \\\\ x & =10\\cdot \\frac{3}{10} \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=3$ into either of the two equations gives $y=-2.$ So, our solution is $x=3$ and $y=-2.$ Therefore, the lines intersect at the point $(3,-2).$\n\nFinally, we find the distance $d$ between the points $(5,-8)$ and $(3,-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(3-5)^{2}+(-2-(-8))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+36} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{40} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the shortest distance between the point $(5,-8)$ and the line $y=\\frac{1}{3}x-3$ is $2\\sqrt{10}.$"} :step/id #uuid "b8b9a1c0-92da-5136-9409-0bd8db22ebdf" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561074}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561096 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342212 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47f7ce0c-16e5-531a-94b9-547622df3f25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-2,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339767} {:answer/id #uuid "569cbb0a-f99c-5355-8f53-bdc678082993" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-4,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339923} {:answer/id #uuid "8688ee8a-8219-5c84-a042-632559c1a6df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-1,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340337} {:answer/id #uuid "95f977c5-80d6-5f10-bfe2-b1560cf968b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-3,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340481} {:answer/id #uuid "ae765e2d-062d-5b17-8c52-1eef7d4ff32e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-5,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340720}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340481} :answer-field/id #uuid "73585de0-1b70-548d-9d76-12f3a61d673b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341582}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7220b7e3-9cce-5102-874c-014511b9e7f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-74439" :question/problem "![](images/1d/1d687bcdc23c283e0945b039781450d2a020f0fd89c0d2976bb252914b296978.png)\n\nFind the point on the line $y+x=-4$ that is closest to the point $(0,2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the equation of the given line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+x & =-4 \\\\ y & =-x-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=-x-4$ and passes through the point $(0,2).$\n\n The given line has a slope of $-1,$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $1.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-2 & =1(x-0) \\\\ y-2 & =x \\\\ y & =x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=x+2 \\\\ y &=-x-4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2 & =-x-4 \\\\ x+x & =-4-2 \\\\ 2x & =-6 \\\\ x & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-3$ into either of the two equations gives $y=-1.$ So, our solution is $x=-3$ and $y=-1.$ So, the lines intersect at the point $(-3,-1).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the point on the line $y+x=-4$ that is closest to the point $(0,2)$ is  $(-3,-1).$"} :step/id #uuid "d242eb63-36df-5068-8499-2c2f42772c0c"}] :activity/title "The Shortest Distance Between a Point and a Line" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560351}]}
